 were second after two legs, trailing Sweden, as " [:a {:href "/athletes/50401"} "Anders Holmertz"] " moved them ahead with the second-fastest split ever, 1:46.16. The Unified Team, all Russians, moved marginally ahead at the last exchange, as " [:a {:href "/athletes/50885"} "Yevgeny Sadovy"] " prepared to swim anchor for them. Sadovy came home over 3½ seconds ahead of Sweden, as the Unified Team set a world record of 7:11.95, better the USA mark of 7:12.51 from Seoul. Sweden hung on for silver. The USA had been fourth for most of the race, but caught Germany on the final leg to earn bronze."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} "Unified Team"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/EUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/EUN.png"}] "EUN"]] [:td {} "7:17.65 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "7:11.95 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:class "bib"} "181"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/50845"} "Dmitry Lepikov"] " "] [:td {}] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:class "bib"} "188"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/50060"} "Vladimir Pyshnenko"] " "] [:td {}] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:class "bib"} "193"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/50913"} "Veniamin Tayanovich"] " "] [:td {}] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:class "bib"} "190"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/50885"} "Yevgeny Sadovy"] " "] [:td {}] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:class "bib"} "